  "note": "Independently reread Section 9.1 and the applicable built-in lexical clauses, all fourteen tagged units, and the cited current producers. The tags discriminate selected integer and module-default representations, decimal64 forms, binary padding/length, bit names, leafref target lexical validation, union-default member selection and both quote styles in instance-identifier leaf-list predicates. The changed validateDecimal64 still checks string lexical form and fraction digits before range. This does not justify the previous whole-requirement enforced verdict. TestRFC7950ReferenceTypesLoad resolves declarations without submitting lexical values. TestRFC7950InstanceIdentifierLeafListPredicateAbsent first accepts the lexical value, then rejects its absent target, so its negative proves require-instance rather than Section 9.1 lexical support. The integer runtime control never exercises unsigned negative zero: validateUnsigned removes only a leading plus and passes minus-zero to ParseUint, refusing a representation of zero admitted by Section 9.2.1's optional plus or minus sign. canonicalValue has the same unsigned parsing boundary. The module-default test already accepts unsigned minus-zero because parseModuleInteger removes the sign and CanonicalModuleDefault normalizes zero. This source-level counterexample was not executed by this reviewer and needs a focused main-thread regression and producer correction; it must not be hidden by restamping enforced. The remaining tagged examples establish their specific cases, not all lexical representations required by Section 9.1. No tests executed by this reviewer.",
